ve biases  may actually improve accuracy in certain  decision scenarios  for instance stephen anderson bennett  found that tasks are relevant for  textual information  a dna match dna unclear or dna does not  match influenced or biased the decisions  of students  when analyzing fingerprint evidence in  both positive  and negative ways  so what they're saying is well if you  have context that leads to more correct  answers then  what's the problem unfortunately  conceptualizing bias in terms of  accuracy and this is a  key point and that's around the nature  of  scientific reasoning so if knowledge  and influence of tasks are relevant to  sexual information  if you have that it really undermines  the independent probative value  of the evidence  that is a fact finder may give the  evidence or opinion  more weight than it deserves  uh so let's say for example the  fingerprints  examiner knows the dna result and  they've incorporated that into  their findings and then in court a  fingerprint result  is presented as independent and a dna  result is presented as a disturbance  the jury or judge does not have no way  of knowing  um how independent that is and how much  how much weight to give each one because  it's being presented as independence  yes it's really not and and this is a  double counting  scenario in addition incorporation of  task irrelevant contextual information  makes assumptions about the correctness  of the extraneous information  and may lead you to explain away  non-corroboratory data  and this is uh what the finding was in  the brandon mayfield case where  um the examiners there were  inconsistencies  in brandon mayfield's fingerprint  versus the front of the crime scene but  they kind of explained those away  because they were already certain that  they should match  uh also using information from another  field goes beyond the area of expertise  so as an example you could ask what is  the true value of the fingerprint  evidence if it is influenced by dna  evidence  or how you know that you were not  influenced by the reference profile  if you did not have the opportunity to  view the crime scene profile  independent of it  so those are important distinctions to  make and it's also important to know  that information does not  equal bias so having the information  doesn't necessarily mean that you'll be  biased by it and also  even today i hear in working group  meetings people saying  a forensic analyst saying but i need  bias to do my job  and i want to make this distinction  because no you don't need  bias to do your job you may need  information but you don't need bias  so information uh is  the information that's used to inform  the decision which can be tasked  relevant or task irrelevant  whereas bias is the undue effect of that  information  so it's when the incidents the  information and  the task of relevant permission has  influenced the decision in  a logical way so there's a distinction  and i think it's important to recognize  that when  uh formulating methods to address it  so when is it appropriate to use  contextual information  the national commission of forensic  sciences put out a  piece in 2015 um  that defines what is task relevant  versus what is task  irrelevant and and  how they define it is it's relevant if  it's necessary for drawing conclusions  about the propositions and questions  from the physical evidence that has been  designated for examination  and through the correct application of  an accepted analytic message  by a competent analyst  it's nice to me here to tell you what is  past relevant and task irrelevant  but it's to make the point that there  are various tasks  during your examination and what may be  relevant in one  may not be relevant in another for  example  uh if you are at the crime scene  collecting that dna evidence then it may  be necessary to know  witness statements to determine where it  may be appropriate to  sample from however  that information is not 